  "worked_solution": "The particle intersects the $x$-axis when its position is $r=\u27e8x,0\u27e9,$ where $x$ is arbitrary. So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e8\\frac{2}{t+1},\\,2t-5\u27e9\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int \\frac{2}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (2t-5)\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t+1)+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}-5t+{C}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=\u27e80,6\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\u27e82\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)+{C}_{1},\\,{0}^{2}-5(0)+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e80,6\u27e9 & =\u27e8{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=6.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\u27e82\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t+1),\\,{t}^{2}-5t+6\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis, we solve for the times at which the $y$-coordinate of the position vector is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-5t+6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t-3) & =0 \\\\ t & =2,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=2$ and $t=3.$",
